      body: "Episode 1 — The Main Course\n\nThe smell of onion in butter has always calmed you. It was like that at twelve, in your grandmother's kitchen in Niterói, and it is like that now, in your small kitchen in Botafogo, with the ceiling fan turning slowly and Teté sitting on the counter, eating olives straight from the jar.\n\n— Say the client's name again — she asked, chewing.\n\n— I already said it three times.\n\n— Say it a fourth. I like watching your face.\n\nYou sighed and stirred the pot.\n\n— Helena Prado.\n\nTeté clapped the way someone claps at a goal.\n\n— Helena. The Helena you cried over, listening to Marisa Monte on my couch. The Helena of \"we want different things, Marina\".\n\n— It's work, Teté. She emailed the site, extremely polite. Dona Lúcia's sixty-first birthday, twelve people, Saturday. She's paying double my usual price.\n\n— She's paying double because she feels guilty.\n\n— She's paying double because it's Saturday and her mother loves my cooking.\n\nThat was true. Dona Lúcia had cried the first time she ate your duck rice. She said it tasted of her mother. You never forgot.\n\nYour phone buzzed on the counter. It was Bia: a voice note, three minutes and forty seconds, and right under it a photo of the sunset in Urca with the caption \"thought of you, the most beautiful thing I know after this\".\n\nYou smiled without meaning to. Teté saw.\n\n— Look at that. Six weeks and already the face of someone eating pudding in secret.\n\n— She's nice.\n\n— \"Nice.\" Have you met her family?\n\n— She said on Saturday she has a family thing, that it's boring, and that on Sunday she'll take me for coffee in Leblon.\n\n— Saturday. Same as your dinner. — Teté took another olive. — What a pretty little coincidence.\n\nYou didn't give it any importance. Not then.",
