      music: "calma",
      body: "The kitchen was hot and fragrant. The duck rested in the low oven, the skin golden, shining with fat. On the stove, the broth for the rice gave off steam that smelled of bay and clove. At the counter, Teté halved cherry tomatoes, one by one, with the patience she only had when there was a knife in her hand.\n\nThe menu was the one Dona Lúcia loved most: salt-cod fritters to start, duck rice with orange for the main course, and, for dessert, tapioca pudding with burnt-coconut syrup.\n\n— You're humming — Teté said.\n\n— I'm not.\n\n— You are. You're singing \"Amor I Love You\". Cooking in your ex's house. I'm going to write a book.\n\nHelena came into the kitchen at that moment, a bottle of wine in her hand, and the two of you went quiet at once, like children caught.\n\n— May I open this one for you? — Helena asked. — It's on the house. You're working, but you can have a glass.\n\n— I'll take it — Teté said, fast.\n\nYou hesitated. Helena poured anyway, and when she handed you the glass, her fingers touched yours. Nothing. Half a second.\n\n— I wanted to tell you something today — she said, low. — Before dinner. About... about who's coming.\n\n— Who's coming?\n\nThe doorbell rang. From the living room, Caio's voice shouted: \"Lena! People are here!\"\n\nHelena took a deep breath.\n\n— I'll tell you later. I promise.\n\nAnd she left.\n\nTeté waited until she disappeared down the hall and turned to you with her eyes wide.\n\n— \"About who's coming.\" Marina, she's going to introduce you to the new girlfriend. I'm sure of it.\n\n— Her problem. I'm with Bia.\n\n— Are you? Or are you just fooling around?\n\nYou didn't answer. You stirred the broth too hard and splashed the stove.",
