      body: "Dona Lúcia arrived the way she always arrived: hair teased high, the smell of hairspray, and more joy than could fit through the door.\n\n— Where's my Marina?\n\nShe walked straight into the kitchen, without asking, and hugged you from the side so she wouldn't crush the dress.\n\n— My girl, I missed you. Have you lost weight? Are you eating? Who cooks for the one who cooks?\n\n— I eat well, Dona Lúcia. Happy birthday.\n\nShe held your face in both hands.\n\n— I never understood why you two ended it. Never. — And she lowered her voice, the way someone tells a secret. — But Helena has a girl now. Pleasant. A photographer. I'm still deciding if I like her.\n\nPhotographer.\n\nThe word hung in the air, but you didn't let it in. Rio de Janeiro has a photographer on every corner. There are more photographers than bakeries.\n\n— I'm glad she's happy — you said.\n\n— Happy, I don't know. But she is.\n\nDona Lúcia left. Teté leaned on the counter, slowly.\n\n— Marina.\n\n— No.\n\n— I was only going to say...\n\n— Teté. It's a coincidence. Let's fry the fritters.\n\nThe oil hissed when the first fritter went into the pan. You concentrated on that: the gold coming up, the smell of salt cod and parsley, the good sound of frying. Outside, the living room filled with voices, laughter, glasses knocking. Someone put Gal Costa on.\n\nAnd, in the middle of all of it, a laugh.\n\nHigh, loose, a little hoarse at the end.\n\nYou knew that laugh. You had heard it on your couch, last Wednesday, while the two of you watched a bad movie and ate popcorn with too much butter.\n\nYour hand stopped, the skimmer in the air.",
