      body: "The platter of fritters weighed more than it should have. You crossed the hall with the work smile on your face, the one you had trained for years at weddings, christenings, and office parties.\n\nThe living room was beautiful. Helena had put white candles on the long table, wildflowers in jam jars, linen napkins folded one by one. Dona Lúcia was laughing at the head of the table. Caio was on the sofa, on his phone. Uncles, cousins, a neighbor in a hat.\n\nAnd, standing beside Helena, a hand at her waist, a woman in a green dress, curly hair pinned up any old way, an old camera hanging from her neck.\n\n— Everyone — Helena said, loud enough to turn the room. — This is Marina, tonight's chef. She's the best cook in Rio. And Marina... this is Bia. My girlfriend.\n\nThe woman in green turned.\n\nFor a second, Bia was still smiling. You saw the exact moment the smile understood before she did. Her eyes went from you to the platter, from the platter to Helena, and came back to you.\n\nShe went white.\n\nThe silence was short, but it was the longest silence of your life. Gal Costa kept singing. Paçoca barked in the yard. Someone asked for more ice.\n\nYou smelled the salt cod, the oil, the candles. You felt the platter slip half a centimeter in your hands and you held on.\n\n— A pleasure — you said. Your voice came out strangely normal. — Salt-cod fritter?\n\nBia opened her mouth. Closed it. Opened it again.\n\nOn the sofa, Caio looked up from his phone for the first time all night. He looked at Bia. He looked at you. And he smiled from the corner of his mouth, slowly, like someone who has just found the best thing on television.\n\nBehind you, in the kitchen doorway, Teté whispered a single word:\n\n— Fuck.",
