      where: "Final — First Page",
      art: "/scenes/cafe.jpg",
      music: "romance",
      body: "Bia answered on the third ring, out of breath, as if she had run for the phone.\n\n— Hi — she said. Only that. Afraid of saying more.\n\n— I'm not going to São Paulo — you said. — I'm going to open my restaurant here. With Teté.\n\n— Marina, that is wonderful. — And she sounded truly happy, even knowing she would travel alone. — It's the best news of the year.\n\n— And I want you to take me to that coffee in Leblon. Before you go. So we start the right way. From the first page.\n\nOn the other end, a silence. Then a sob and a laugh mixed together.\n\n— Tomorrow? At nine? I'll be on time. I swear. I'll set four alarms.\n\nShe arrived ten minutes late. A persimmon in her hand, as a gift. You laughed so hard the waiter asked if you were all right.\n\nAt that coffee, she told you everything. The good things and the dull ones. That she had been afraid of being alone since she was a teenager, and that was why she stayed in things that were already over. That she had started therapy on Tuesday. That she had hated olives since she was six, because of an uncle who made her eat them.\n\n— Nobody in photography hates olives — she said. — For the record.\n\n— I know. It was the worst lie of my life.\n\nShe left for São Paulo three days later. And for three months she sent one message a day, and missed none. No four-minute voice notes. One message, and a truth in it. \"Today I was afraid of disappointing you.\" \"Today I missed the way you smell of onion.\" \"Today I photographed a plate and thought yours was more beautiful.\"",
