        title: "Table for One",
        flower: "Honey light at the window",
        body: "Helena sent the blue folder with a note: \"No conditions. As I promised.\" You used one drawing only: the chalkboard on the wall, the menu written by hand.\n\nThe name was not Casa Marina. It was Mesa Posta. Because that was what you wanted: a table always set for whoever came.\n\nAt the opening, Dona Lúcia was the first customer, hair teased high and a new hat. She ordered duck rice. She cried. Aunt Glorinha arrived next, half of Rio de Janeiro already warned. Caio came with Davi, and the two of them sat at the counter, hands held. Rodrigo showed up, tasted everything, and left saying he was going to steal your farofa.\n\nHelena arrived in a dress the color of sand and smiled from across the room. Bia arrived late, a persimmon in her hand. The two of them sat at the same table, talked about photography and architecture, and by the end of the night they were laughing together at something you never learned. You thought it was the most beautiful thing in the world.\n\nAt midnight, when the last guest had gone and Teté had left singing down the middle of the street, you stayed alone in the room.\n\nYou washed a skillet. You put in a little butter. You chopped an onion slowly, your grandmother's way. The smell rose and filled the whole restaurant. You made an egg, only one, and a little of the rice that was left.\n\nYou set a plate on the counter, facing the big window. A napkin. A glass of wine.\n\nYou sat.\n\nTable for one. And never, in all your life, had you felt so accompanied.",
